\name{InferenceSurvivalRestrictedMeanDiff}
\alias{InferenceSurvivalRestrictedMeanDiff}
\title{Restricted Mean Survival Time (RMST) Difference Inference for Survival Responses}
\description{
Fits a non-parametric treatment-effect estimator for censored survival
responses: the difference in \strong{restricted mean survival time} (RMST)
between the treated and control arms, \eqn{\hat\mu_T(\tau) - \hat\mu_C(\tau)},
where both arms use the same truncation horizon \eqn{\tau}. By default
\eqn{\tau} is the smaller of the two arms' maximum follow-up times; it can
be fixed by the user (e.g. \code{tau = 36} for "RMST at 36 months") via the
\code{tau} constructor argument. The horizon is set once, from the observed
data (or the user's \code{tau}), and then held fixed in every randomization,
randomization-bootstrap, bootstrap and jackknife draw; it is not recomputed
from each draw's arms. A horizon recomputed per draw would depend on the
assignment, so the null distribution would compare RMSTs over different
horizons than the observed statistic and the randomization test could lose
almost all its power. Prespecifying \code{tau} in the trial protocol (for
example the planned follow-up) is still recommended: the default horizon is
itself data-dependent. Each RMST is the area under its
Kaplan-Meier survival curve up to that horizon
(\eqn{\hat\mu(\tau) = \int_0^\tau \hat S(t)\,dt}),
computed by exact integration of the step-function KM curve (a sum of rectangles). The
standard error of the difference comes from the Greenwood-type variance of
each arm's RMST, combined across the two (independent) arms via
\code{get_restricted_mean_se_diff()}. When that standard error is
unavailable or non-finite, \code{$compute_asymp_confidence_interval()} and
\code{$compute_asymp_two_sided_pval()} return \code{NA}, with the reason
recorded and an \code{edi_wald_unavailable_warning}; before EDI 1.0.6 they
fell back to a nonparametric bootstrap (v1.0.6-TODO-380). On left- or
interval-censored data, which have no analytic standard error, they return
the nonparametric bootstrap interval and p-value.
Randomization confidence intervals are not supported (the RMST-difference
units are not commensurate with the randomization CI bisection algorithm's
transformed-scale null search). Randomization-bootstrap confidence intervals
(every type) are refused for the same reason: they invert a log-time shift,
so their bounds are for a log-time ratio. The randomization and
randomization-bootstrap p-values at \eqn{\delta = 0} remain available.

\strong{The standard error assumes independent arms.} The Greenwood-type
variance treats the two arms as independent samples, which is correct for
Bernoulli assignment and complete randomization (\code{DesignSeqOneByOneBernoulli},
\code{DesignFixedBernoulli}, \code{DesignFixediBCRD},
\code{DesignSeqOneByOneiBCRD}).
Under every other design (blocking, stratified permuted blocks, matching,
KK pairs, rerandomization, covariate-adaptive and other balancing designs) it
ignores the balance the design enforces and is \strong{conservative}: Wald
intervals over-cover and Wald p-values are too large. Randomization tests and
bootstrap intervals that resample according to the design do not share this
issue. Design-aware variances are planned.

\strong{Left- or interval-censored data.} The survival curve then comes from
a Turnbull NPMLE (\code{interval::icfit()}) rather than Kaplan-Meier. Exact
events are folded in as degenerate intervals \eqn{[y, y]} (they are not
dropped), right-censored subjects are \eqn{[y_L, \infty)}, and any row that
cannot be used is excluded with a warning. Both arms are integrated to one
shared horizon \eqn{\tau}: the user's \code{tau}, or by default the smaller
of the two arms' largest finite observed endpoints. Within a Turnbull
interval the curve is not identified; it is taken to step down at the
interval's left endpoint. An arm with no events before \eqn{\tau} has RMST
equal to \eqn{\tau}. The Bayesian bootstrap
(\code{$compute_bayesian_bootstrap_confidence_interval()},
\code{$compute_bayesian_bootstrap_two_sided_pval()} and
\code{$approximate_bayesian_bootstrap_distribution_beta_hat_T()}) stops
with an error on left- or interval-censored data, because \code{icfit()} has no weights argument; use the
nonparametric bootstrap there instead. On right-censored data it is
unaffected.
}
\section{Small-sample behaviour}{

How fast the size (and coverage) error of each procedure is expected to vanish as the
effective sample size \eqn{n_{eff}}{n_eff} grows, from EDI's convergence-rate registry
(\code{inference_convergence_expectation()}). \emph{Exact} procedures are valid at every
n by construction; the others are asymptotic, so a small-sample excess is expected, and
its constant is not given by an order. The table is the expectation for a balanced
complete-randomization design; it is not a measured size. Reference keys are explained
under References.

\tabular{lllllll}{\strong{Procedure} \tab \strong{Regime} \tab \strong{Error order} \tab \strong{Effective n} \tab \strong{Regularity} \tab \strong{Basis} \tab \strong{References} \cr
\code{rand} \tab finite sample \tab exact \tab subjects \tab regular \tab theory \tab [Fisher1935], [LehmannRomano2005] \cr
\code{wald} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} \tab subjects \tab regular \tab assumed default \tab [Hall1992] \cr
\code{jackknife} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} \tab subjects \tab regular \tab assumed default \tab [ShaoTu1995] \cr
\code{bootstrap} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} \tab subjects \tab regular \tab assumed default \tab [Hall1992] \cr
\code{bayes_boot} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} \tab subjects \tab regular \tab assumed default \tab [Rubin1981], [Lo1987] \cr
\code{rand_bootstrap} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} \tab subjects \tab regular \tab assumed default \tab [Hall1992], [Kallus2018]}

The point estimate's bias is of order \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} (assumed default: [CoxSnell1968]).

Rows with basis \emph{assumed default}: the order shown is the procedure's rate in a
regular parametric model, and the cited works establish it for that case only. EDI has not
derived it for this class.

Design-specific differences from the table:
\itemize{
\item On any design with unequal allocation (\code{prob_T} not 0.5), for \code{wald}, \code{jackknife}, \code{bootstrap}, \code{bayes_boot}, \code{rand_bootstrap}: the effective n is the size of the smaller arm.
\item On \code{DesignFixedBlockedCluster}, \code{DesignFixedCluster}, for \code{wald}, \code{jackknife}, \code{bootstrap}, \code{bayes_boot}, \code{rand_bootstrap}: the effective n is the number of clusters.
}
}

